= Photosynthesis =

== Overview ==
'''Photosynthesis''' consists of biological processes that use light energy to drive chemical reactions and, in many organisms, to fix inorganic carbon into organic compounds. In oxygenic photosynthesis, carried out by plants, algae and cyanobacteria, water supplies electrons and molecular oxygen is released. Other photosynthetic microorganisms can use different electron donors and need not release oxygen.

== Light-driven electron transfer ==
In oxygenic photosynthesis, light absorption by pigments initiates electron transfer through membrane-associated complexes. Water oxidation supplies electrons, releases oxygen and helps establish an electrochemical proton gradient. The resulting energy conversion supports synthesis of ATP and reduction of NADP+ to NADPH.

In plants and many algae, these reactions occur in chloroplast thylakoid membranes. In cyanobacteria, the photosynthetic membranes have a different cellular organization. The mechanism depends on the organism and should not be reduced to a universal single chloroplast pathway.

== Carbon fixation ==
Carbon-fixing reactions can use ATP and reducing power generated by the light reactions to incorporate carbon dioxide into organic molecules. In plants, the Calvin–Benson cycle is an important pathway. The shorthand 'dark reactions' is potentially misleading: these reactions do not directly absorb photons, but they can be regulated by light and depend on products of light-driven chemistry.

== Energy and carbon-cycle roles ==
[[Related to::Energy conversion|Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical potential energy]]. It contributes to the [[Related to::Carbon cycle|carbon cycle]] by transferring carbon from inorganic pools into biomass. Carbon fixation and oxygen production are related in oxygenic organisms, but the exact stoichiometry and fate of products depend on environmental conditions and metabolism.

== Regulation and evidence ==
Rates respond to light, carbon dioxide availability, water, temperature, nutrients and species-specific physiology. Experimentally distinguishing gross photosynthetic fixation from net ecosystem carbon accumulation requires accounting for respiration, downstream transport and losses. An increase in leaf-level photosynthesis does not alone establish an enduring atmospheric carbon sink.

== Boundaries ==
Not all photosynthetic organisms are plants, not all carbon fixation requires light, and not all photosynthesis releases oxygen. Photosynthesis is also not equivalent to the complete energy budget of an organism.

